from flask import Flask, render_template, request
from flask_sqlalchemy import SQLAlchemy
from datetime import datetime 

Imports: Imports necessary modules and classes to create a Flask web application, SQLAlchemy for database integration, and datetime for timestamps.



app = Flask(__name__)
app.config['SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI'] = 'sqlite:///db.db'
app.config['SQLALCHEMY_TRACK_MODIFICATIONS'] = False
db = SQLAlchemy(app)

App Initialization and Configuration: Creates a Flask application, configures SQLAlchemy to use a SQLite database, and disables unnecessary modification tracking.



class Message(db.Model):
    id = db.Column(db.Integer, primary_key=True)
    user = db.Column(db.String(200), nullable=True)
    content = db.Column(db.String(500), nullable=True)
    created_at = db.Column(db.DateTime(), default=datetime.now)

Database Model: Defines a Message model with fields id, user, content, and created_at for storing messages in the database.
Here I had the problem that the class created.at datetime.utcnow was obsolete, so I had to change it to datetime.now,
but unfortunately I could no longer load this into my database.




@app.route("/<name>", methods=['GET', 'POST'])
def start_page(name):   
    if request.method == 'POST':
        new_message = Message(
            user=name,
            content=request.form['content']
        )
        db.session.add(new_message)
        db.session.commit()
    messages = Message.query.order_by(Message.id).all()
    return render_template('index.html', messages=messages, name=name)

Route and View Function: Defines a route for the URL /<name>. If a POST request is received, 
a new message is created and added to the database. Then, all messages are retrieved and the index.html 
template is rendered with the messages and the user's name.


App Launch: Starts the Flask application in debug mode if the script is executed directly.Debug Mode: In debug mode, the server provides 
detailed error messages and automatically reloads the application when changes are detected in the code, making development and debugging easier.
